o(7,3,'explanation','The public key is presented for verification and is not secret. Authorisation may use authorized_keys, certificates or another configured source.')
u(7,['workedExample'],'The first key-exchange hash binds the authentication signature to this session. A different session has a different exchange hash with overwhelming probability, so simply replaying that signature or relaying it across two separately negotiated sessions fails. Host-key verification still matters: trusting an attacker as the server exposes commands/data sent to that endpoint. That alone does not give the attacker authentication to the real server; agent forwarding or stolen credentials would be additional capabilities.')
u(9,['prompt'],'Ten healthy gRPC backends sit behind a balancer that chooses a backend once per TCP connection. Four client instances each use exactly one persistent connection. The four connections land on four different backends, which receive all the traffic while six remain idle. What explains this?')
